<Layouts.app flash={@flash} current_scope={@current_scope} max_width="max-w-4xl">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-10 space-y-10">
    <header class="space-y-4">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">How to use FaB Tabletop</h1>
      <p class="text-lg">
        FaB Tabletop is a free platform for playing Flesh and Blood TCG online
        with live video, life tracking, and combat chain management. This guide
        walks you through camera setup, the lobby, and the in-game screen with
        examples from real FaB play.
      </p>
      <nav class="text-base">
        <span class="font-semibold">Jump to:</span>
        <a href="#camera-setup" class="link link-primary mx-2">Camera setup</a>
        <a href="#lobby" class="link link-primary mx-2">Lobby</a>
        <a href="#in-game" class="link link-primary mx-2">In-game</a>
        <a href="#contact" class="link link-primary mx-2">Contact</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <%!-- =================================================================
          SECTION 1 — CAMERA SETUP
         ================================================================= --%>
    <section id="camera-setup" class="space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold border-b border-base-300 pb-2">1. Camera setup</h2>
      <p class="text-lg">
        Before joining your first game, configure your camera so your opponent
        can see your playmat and cards clearly. Your zoom and rotation settings
        are saved in your browser — you only need to do this once.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        It is important to get this right. Be sure that you are in a well lit
        environment, but no direct lighting on the cards as the glare can make
        them hard to read. Center your playmat in the preview and zoom in so
        your hero card and pitch zone are clearly visible. Set the rotation so
        that the cards are orientated on your screen as you would read them on
        a physical playmat.
      </p>

      <%!-- 1.1 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">1.1 Framing your play area (zoom & rotation)</h3>
        <p>
          The camera setup screen previews exactly what your opponent will see.
          Use <strong>Rotate</strong>
          if your camera is mounted sideways or upside-down — most overhead
          rigs end up somewhere near 180° — then use <strong>Zoom</strong>
          to fill the frame with your playmat and crop out the desk around it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Aim to have your whole playmat in frame with a little room to spare:
          your pitch zone, arsenal and hero card should all be readable without
          your opponent squinting. Both sliders save to your browser when you
          click <strong>Save & Continue</strong>, so every future game starts
          with the same framing.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/1-1-camera-setup.gif"}
            alt="Adjusting rotation and zoom on the camera setup screen"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            Camera setup, start to finish — the preview updates as you drag.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <%!-- 1.2 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">
          1.2 Using your phone as a camera
          <span class="text-base font-normal text-base-content/60">(optional)</span>
        </h3>
        <p>
          If your laptop webcam can't see the table, or if you'd prefer, you
          can use your phone as a camera instead. Open the settings dialog and
          scan the QR code under <strong>Phone Camera</strong>
          with your phone, accept the camera permission on the phone, then
          switch <strong>Camera Source</strong>
          to <strong>Phone</strong>
          once the badge reads "Phone connected".
        </p>
        <p>
          Your phone stays on the relay page for the rest of the match, with
          its own zoom slider and a button to flip between the front and rear
          cameras. Streaming video is hard work for a phone, so keep it plugged
          in — and mount it somewhere it won't get knocked while you play.
        </p>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2">
            <img
              src={~p"/images/about/1-2-settings-phone-connected.webp"}
              alt="Settings dialog with the phone camera QR code and Phone connected badge"
              class="w-full rounded"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
              Scan the QR code, then switch the camera source to Phone.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2">
            <img
              src={~p"/images/about/1-2-phone-view.webp"}
              alt="Phone showing the camera relay page with zoom and flip controls"
              class="w-full rounded"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
              Your phone shows the relay page — keep it open during your game.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>

      <%!-- 1.3 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">1.3 Where your tiles land</h3>
        <p>
          Every tracker you turn on — Physical damage, Arcane damage, Amp, Go
          Again, on-hit effects, custom counters — appears as a tile drawn over
          your video feed, and your opponent sees it in the same spot you do.
          Tiles are draggable, so you can drop each one somewhere that matches
          how you arrange your physical playmat. Putting the Physical tile near
          the slot where you place attack cards, for example, makes on-hit
          damage easy to read at a glance.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tiles stack neatly when several are active at once, which is what a
          typical attack looks like: the damage on the chain, the on-hit
          effects riding along with it, and whether the attack has Go Again.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2 max-w-2xl">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/1-3-stacking-effects.webp"}
            alt="Physical, Bloodrot Pox, Lose Health and Go Again tiles stacked over the playmat"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            A Kiss of Death with a Spike with Bloodrot reaction: 3 physical, Bloodrot Pox, lose 1 health, and Go Again (Slippy ability).
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%!-- =================================================================
          SECTION 2 — LOBBY / MAIN SCREEN
         ================================================================= --%>
    <section id="lobby" class="space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold border-b border-base-300 pb-2">2. The lobby</h2>
      <p class="text-lg">
        The lobby is where you'll start every session. From here you can
        browse open games, create your own, join a private game by code, or
        rejoin a game already in progress. The <strong>Live activity</strong>
        column on the right shows how busy the site is right now — games
        waiting for an opponent, what people are playing this week, upcoming
        tournaments, and recent winners with their decklists.
      </p>

      <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2">
        <img
          src={~p"/images/about/2-0-lobby.webp"}
          alt="The lobby with Games to join, Create Game and Live activity columns"
          class="w-full rounded"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
          The lobby at a glance: games to join, create a game, and live activity.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <%!-- 2.1 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">2.1 Browsing public games</h3>
        <p>
          Public games are grouped by format — Classic Constructed, Blitz, and
          so on — with a count next to each. Expand a format to see who's
          waiting: you get the game title, the host's name, their language, and
          their hero, plus a link to their decklist if they shared one. Hit <strong>Join</strong>
          and you'll go straight to camera setup, then into the game.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2 max-w-2xl">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/2-1-join-a-game.webp"}
            alt="Expanded list of public Classic Constructed games with a Join button"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            Open games, grouped by format.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <%!-- 2.2 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">2.2 Creating a game</h3>
        <p>
          To host, pick a <strong>Format</strong>, give the game a <strong>Title</strong>
          (this is what other players see in the lobby), choose your <strong>Hero</strong>, and optionally paste a Fabrary
          <strong>Decklist</strong>
          link so your opponent can check what you're bringing. Then hit <strong>Start</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two toggles change who can find you. <strong>Private game</strong>
          keeps the game out of the public list — share the game code from the
          in-game settings dialog instead. <strong>Competitive</strong>
          leaves the game public but hides your hero from the listing, so you
          don't tip your matchup before the match starts. If you just want to
          run it back, <strong>Quick match</strong>
          at the top reuses the settings from your last game.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2 max-w-md">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/2-2-create-game.gif"}
            alt="Filling out and submitting the create-game form"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            Creating a new Classic Constructed game.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <%!-- 2.3 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">2.3 Joining a private game</h3>
        <p>
          To join a private game, ask the host for their game code — they can
          copy it from their in-game settings dialog. Click <strong>Join private</strong>
          in the lobby and paste it in. The full game link works too, so a host
          can just send you the URL.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2 max-w-2xl">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/2-3-join-private-game.webp"}
            alt="Join private game modal with the game code field"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            Paste the host's game code or link to join.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <%!-- 2.4 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">2.4 Resuming a game in progress</h3>
        <p>
          If you're already in a game, a <strong>Game in Progress</strong>
          panel appears at the top of the lobby with the game's title and
          format. <strong>Rejoin Game</strong>
          drops you back in — handy after a refresh, a dropped connection, or
          closing the tab by accident. <strong>Leave Game</strong>
          bows out for good and frees your opponent up to find someone else.
        </p>
        <p>
          You have a five minute grace period after disconnecting before you're
          marked as having left, so a browser crash mid-match isn't the end of
          the game.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/2-4-resume-game.webp"}
            alt="Game in Progress panel with Rejoin Game and Leave Game buttons"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            Rejoin or leave a game from the lobby.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%!-- =================================================================
          SECTION 3 — IN-GAME SCREEN
         ================================================================= --%>
    <section id="in-game" class="space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold border-b border-base-300 pb-2">3. In-game screen</h2>
      <p class="text-lg">
        The in-game screen is where every match happens. Your opponent's
        playmat fills the middle of the screen, your own preview sits up in the
        corner, and your tracker sidebar runs down the left — damage, effects,
        tokens, card search, and your life total.
      </p>

      <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2">
        <img
          src={~p"/images/about/3-0-game-board.webp"}
          alt="The in-game screen with the tracker sidebar and the opponent's playmat"
          class="w-full rounded"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
          The in-game screen: tracker sidebar on the left, opponent's board in the middle.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <%!-- 3.1 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">
          3.1 Top bar: camera, mic, sound, connection, settings, leave
        </h3>
        <p>
          The top bar holds your own camera preview on the far left, then
          toggles for your <strong>camera</strong>, your <strong>microphone</strong>, and your
          <strong>speaker</strong>
          (mute your opponent without muting yourself). In the middle is the
          game title and your opponent's hero. On the right sits the connection
          badge — <strong>Connecting</strong>, <strong>Connected</strong>
          or <strong>Disconnected</strong>
          — then the settings gear and the leave-game button. If your opponent
          turns off their camera or mic, an indicator appears on their feed.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/3-1-top-bar.webp"}
            alt="In-game top bar controls"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            The in-game top bar.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <%!-- 3.2 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">3.2 Life total</h3>
        <p>
          Your life total sits at the bottom of the sidebar with +/- buttons,
          and your opponent's is mirrored in the corner of their board. Most
          Classic Constructed heroes start at 40 — when an opponent's <em>Snatch</em>
          hits you for 4, tap minus four times to drop from 40 to 36. Life is
          the one thing <strong>Reset Board</strong>
          never touches, so it carries across the whole match.
        </p>
        <%!-- TODO: capture a life-counter clip — using +/- to go 40 → 36 → 35
              in response to damage, with the opponent's mirrored total
              updating at the same time. --%>
      </div>

      <%!-- 3.3 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">3.3 Combat chain management</h3>
        <p>
          The top of the sidebar is your combat chain. Toggle <strong>Physical</strong>
          or <strong>Arcane</strong>
          when an attack is incoming and set the amount with +/-; your opponent
          sees the number update live, so they know exactly what's on the chain
          before it resolves. A 5-power attack met with 2 block and no armour
          means you set Physical to 3. Dual-damage chains just use both tiles at
          once.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Amp</strong>
          carries its own counter for the X in <em>Amp X</em>, and <strong>Go Again</strong>
          is a straight toggle — flip it when your attack grants it, so your
          opponent knows another action is coming rather than waiting on you.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the chain closes, <strong>Reset Board</strong>
          clears everything you had lit up — damage, Amp, Go Again, abilities,
          on-hit effects and custom counters — in one click. Your life total and
          proxy tokens survive, since those carry between chains.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/3-3-basic-combat-chain.gif"}
            alt="Setting physical damage and an on-hit effect, resolving the hit, then resetting the board"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            A chain link start to finish: set the damage, resolve the hit, reset the board.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <%!-- 3.4 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">3.4 On-hit effects</h3>
        <p>
          The <strong>On Hits</strong>
          dropdown tracks everything that triggers off a successful hit —
          Bloodrot Pox, Frostbite, Frailty, Inertia, Mark, card draw, discard,
          banishing from arsenal or graveyard, and plenty more. Effects with a
          variable amount (Lose Health, Deal Damage, Deal Arcane Damage, Gain
          Health) have their own +/- so you can set the X; the rest are simple
          toggles.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each one you tick becomes a tile on your opponent's view, and hovering
          it shows the reminder text — useful when they haven't met that card
          before. <strong>Create Token…</strong>
          at the bottom opens a second panel for the tokens you make for
          yourself: Runechants, Embers, Seismic Surge, Spectral Shield, and the
          rest.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2 max-w-lg">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/3-4-on-hits.webp"}
            alt="On Hits dropdown with effects ticked and counters set"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            Toggle effects on hit, and set the X on the counterable ones.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <%!-- 3.5 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">3.5 Tokens & counters</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Create Proxy Token</strong>
          hands your opponent a token they'd otherwise have to remember —
          Mark, Bloodrot Pox, Frailty, Frostbite, Inertia and Gold. Pick one
          from the dropdown and it shows up in their proxy token panel as a
          full card image, so they can read exactly what it does. Counters use
          +/- for the ones that stack; Mark is a single toggle since you can
          only ever be marked once.
        </p>
        <p>
          For anything the app doesn't have a tile for, type a name into the
          <strong>Counter…</strong>
          box and hit +. That drops a named counter onto your board that you can
          increment, decrement, and drag wherever you like — good for one-off
          card effects and hero-specific tracking.
        </p>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 items-start">
          <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2">
            <img
              src={~p"/images/about/3-5-create-proxy-token.webp"}
              alt="Create Proxy Token dropdown listing Mark, Frailty, Inertia, Bloodrot Pox, Frostbite and Gold"
              class="w-full rounded"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
              Pick a token to give your opponent.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2">
            <img
              src={~p"/images/about/3-5-proxy-token.webp"}
              alt="Proxy token panel showing a Marked token card"
              class="w-full rounded"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
              They see the token as a card, and it stays until dismissed.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>

      <%!-- 3.6 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">3.6 Click a card to recognise it</h3>
        <p>
          Can't read a card on your opponent's mat? Click it. FaB Tabletop
          grabs that frame, finds the card in it, and matches it against the
          full card database — the high-resolution card image pops out on top
          of the board a moment later, no typing required.
        </p>
        <p>
          Recognition runs entirely in your browser, so it doesn't cost your
          opponent anything and works while they're mid-turn. Pop-outs are
          draggable and you can open as many as you like, which makes it easy to
          line up a couple of cards and compare them. If the match isn't quite
          right, the dropdown under the image offers the other candidates, and
          the coloured dots switch between pitch variants. Cards lying sideways
          on the mat work fine, and so do cards facing away from you.
        </p>
        <p>
          A scan needs a reasonably clear look at the card — heavy glare, a
          hand covering the art, or a card at a steep angle can all defeat it.
          If nothing opens, nudge the card into better light and try again, or
          fall back to the search box below.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/3-6-card-recognition-search.gif"}
            alt="Clicking cards on the opponent's playmat to pop out the matching card images"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            Click a card on the board and it pops out, full size and readable.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <%!-- 3.7 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">3.7 Card search</h3>
        <p>
          The <strong>Search card…</strong>
          box looks up any FaB card by name. It's forgiving of typos and
          half-remembered names, so "scar for" is enough to find <em>Scar for a Scar</em>. The result pops out the same way a scanned
          card does — draggable, closable, and readable by both of you.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every pop-out doubles as a search box: edit the name in its header to
          swap it for a different card without opening another one. The
          coloured dots underneath jump between pitch variants, so you can show
          your opponent the red, yellow, and blue versions of a card in a
          couple of clicks.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2 max-w-2xl">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/3-7-card-search.gif"}
            alt="Typing a card name into the search box and popping out the result"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            Live card search — type a name to pop out the card.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <%!-- 3.8 --%>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">3.8 In-game settings</h3>
        <p>
          The settings dialog covers everything you might need to change
          mid-match:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>
            <strong>Flip opponent's view</strong>
            — rotate their feed 180° if their camera is mounted the other way up.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Card scan debug overlay</strong>
            — shows what the card scanner actually sees. Handy if recognition is
            misbehaving and you want to report why.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Effect volume</strong>
            and <strong>Opponent volume</strong>
            — independent sliders for the app's sound effects and your
            opponent's microphone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Share game</strong> — copy the game code so someone can join a private game.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Camera Source</strong>
            — switch between your webcam and your phone, with the QR code and
            connection badge right below.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Reconfigure Camera</strong>
            — jump back to camera setup to re-do your zoom and rotation.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <figure class="rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-2 max-w-md">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/about/3-8-settings-dialog.webp"}
            alt="In-game settings dialog"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/70 pt-2">
            In-game settings: view flip, volumes, share code, camera source.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%!-- =================================================================
          SECTION 4 — CONTACT (preserved from original About page)
         ================================================================= --%>
    <section id="contact" class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold border-b border-base-300 pb-2">Contact</h2>
      <p class="text-lg">
        Have questions or feedback? Reach out on
        <a href="https://github.com/IAmThermite/fab-tabletop" class="link link-primary">GitHub</a>
        or drop us a message on Discord.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layouts.app>
